      <p>Add $6k&ndash;$12k for a trailer, or $4k&ndash;$20k for a foundation. Loft square footage is usually priced at about half, since it adds framing and finish but no kitchen, bath or extra wall height in most designs. If you are unsure what size to plan for, the <a href="/tools/how-big-should-my-tiny-house-be/">tiny house size calculator</a> works it out from your household.</p>

      <h2 id="components">Cost to build a tiny house, by component</h2>
      <p>Here is where the money goes on a typical 200 sq ft DIY tiny house on wheels. The low column is a basic, careful build; the high column is a well-finished house with better windows, appliances and a tile shower.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>Component</th><th class="num">Low</th><th class="num">High</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Trailer (new, tiny-house-specific)</td><td class="num">$6,000</td><td class="num">$12,000</td><td>Length, axle rating and flange-free decks drive price</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Framing lumber, sheathing, fasteners</td><td class="num">$4,000</td><td class="num">$6,500</td><td>Steel framing and SIPs cost more</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Roofing and underlayment</td><td class="num">$1,500</td><td class="num">$3,000</td><td>Standing-seam metal is the usual choice</td></tr>
